name: your own tools
intro: Real unix tools are small C programs. `wc`, `uniq`, `tac`, `xxd` --
intro: each does one thing on a stream of bytes, and you now know every
intro: part of them. This unit is four such tools, rebuilt from the spec.
intro:
intro: They share one shape, the shape of every filter:
intro:
intro:     no argument   read standard input
intro:     one argument  read that file
intro:     bad usage     message to stderr, exit 2
intro:     cannot open   message to stderr, exit 1
intro:
intro: The trick is that `stdin` is already a `FILE *`, so one function
intro: serves both:
intro:
intro:     FILE *in = stdin;
intro:     if (argc == 2) {
intro:         in = fopen(argv[1], "rb");
intro:         if (in == NULL) { ...error, exit 1... }
intro:     }
intro:     /* ... read from `in`, then: */
intro:     if (in != stdin) fclose(in);
intro:
intro: Never fclose stdin. Read bytes with `getc` until it returns EOF --
intro: keep it in an `int`, not a `char`, or it breaks: where char is
intro: signed (x86) a real 0xff byte looks like EOF; where it is unsigned
intro: (ARM) EOF never arrives and the loop runs forever. Read in blocks
intro: with `fread`. Past that, the
intro: format in each spec is a contract: the same bytes in must give the
intro: same bytes out, exactly, every time. When the spec names a message,
intro: print that message, not the strerror one.
intro:
intro: The last program has to hold the whole input before it can print
intro: the first line of its answer, so it grows a buffer with `realloc`.
intro: Ask for double each time; check every call; free at the end.
---
q: wcount.c -- count lines, words and bytes
spec: With no argument read standard input; with one, read that file. Print
spec: one line, `lines=L words=W bytes=B`, and exit 0.
spec:
spec: L is the number of newline characters, so a last line with no
spec: newline is not counted. A word is a run of characters that are not
spec: whitespace (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab, form
spec: feed). B is every byte. Empty input prints `lines=0 words=0 bytes=0`.
spec:
spec: If the file cannot be opened print `wcount: cannot open NAME` to
spec: standard error and exit 1. More than one argument prints
spec: `usage: wcount [FILE]` and exits 2.
file: wcount.c
prog: #include <ctype.h>
prog: #include <stdbool.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	if (argc > 2) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: wcount [FILE]\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	FILE *in = stdin;
prog: 	if (argc == 2) {
prog: 		in = fopen(argv[1], "rb");
prog: 		if (in == NULL) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "wcount: cannot open %s\n", argv[1]);
prog: 			return 1;
prog: 		}
prog: 	}
prog: 	size_t lines = 0, words = 0, bytes = 0;
prog: 	bool inword = false;
prog: 	int c;
prog: 	while ((c = getc(in)) != EOF) {
prog: 		bytes++;
prog: 		if (c == '\n')
prog: 			lines++;
prog: 		if (isspace(c)) {
prog: 			inword = false;
prog: 		} else if (!inword) {
prog: 			inword = true;
prog: 			words++;
prog: 		}
prog: 	}
prog: 	int status = ferror(in) ? 1 : 0;
prog: 	if (in != stdin)
prog: 		fclose(in);
prog: 	if (status != 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "wcount: read error\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	printf("lines=%zu words=%zu bytes=%zu\n", lines, words, bytes);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: printf 'a b\nc\n' | wcount
case: printf 'no newline here' | wcount
case: printf '' | wcount
case: printf '  \n\t\n a\tb  c \n' | wcount
case: printf 'x y\n' > f; wcount f
case: wcount nothere; echo $?
case: wcount a b; echo $?
hint: getc into an int until EOF; a word starts when a non-space follows a space (or the start), so remember whether you are inside one
